THE GC CONSTITUTION
Articles I–III

Article I — Protection of Soundboards

No member may delete any soundboard without authorization.
Special protection applies to soundboards created for a specific member or containing their voice, words, or personalized content.
Anyone who deletes a soundboard shall be permanently prohibited from submitting any new soundboards for acceptance.
If the deleted soundboard was created by the offender or specifically belongs to them, a personal soundboard associated with the offender shall also be added as an additional penalty alongside the initial punishment.

Article II — Right to Legal Representation

Every member has the right to appoint a lawyer to defend them against any proposed or imposed penalty.
The lawyer may present arguments, evidence, and defenses on behalf of the accused member.
No member shall be denied legal representation during disciplinary proceedings.
Appointing a lawyer does not automatically suspend or cancel a penalty.

Article III — The Server’s Lawyers

GC shall have two official lawyers responsible for defending the server in disputes concerning penalties and disciplinary decisions.
They shall present evidence, explain the grounds for penalties, and defend the server’s position before the judges.
Both lawyers must operate according to this Constitution.
Articles IV–VI
Article IV — The Right to Challenge Penalties

Every member has the right to challenge or appeal a penalty they believe is unjustified.
Challenges must follow the established dispute-resolution process.
The judges shall examine the evidence and arguments presented by both parties.
Good-faith challenges shall not, by themselves, constitute an offense.

Article V — Unfounded Challenges

If the judges determine that a challenge to a penalty is unfounded, an additional penalty shall be imposed on the member who made the challenge.
The additional penalty shall depend on the circumstances and seriousness of the violation.
An unsuccessful appeal alone does not prove that the challenge was made in bad faith.
The member retains the right to legal representation.

Article VI — Challenges to the Credibility of the Owner and Judges

Repeated, unjustified challenges to the credibility of the GC Owner concerning disciplinary decisions may result in an additional penalty.
Repeated, unjustified challenges to the integrity or impartiality of the judges may also result in an additional penalty.
Friendly jokes, ordinary banter, respectful disagreement, and good-faith criticism shall not constitute violations.
The judges shall consider the context and available evidence before imposing any penalty.
Final Provision

This Constitution shall serve as the official governing document of GC. All members, lawyers, judges, and the Owner shall abide by its provisions. No amendments shall be made unless officially announced.

Principle of Factual Judgment: Decisions and penalties shall be based solely on what actually occurred, the established facts, and the available evidence, rather than presumed intentions or personal interpretations. Violations shall be judged according to the actions that took place, not what a person is assumed to have intended. 

